From: graps@clio.arc.nasa.gov (Amara Graps)
Subject: A Letter from a Latvian Astronomer

Dear Extro-Beings:

I received a letter recently from a Latvian Astronomer named Juris Zagars
who spent about 1 1/2 days 2 years ago showing me many many interesting 
things and places in Riga, Latvia (capital of Latvia). I was there in July 
1991 for a scientific conference and to visit relatives.  I have not been back 
there since.  As you may recall, the coup was in August 1991, and Latvia
gained their independence.  I would love to see the changes.

This correspondence is the first I've heard from him since July 1991, 
and I consider it a touching and interesting summary of what has happened
to the scientific community of a post-Soviet country.  I hope you enjoy
this personal insight.

Amara Graps
graps@gal.arc.nasa.gov

P.S. I'm typing it in exactly as he wrote it to me because I thought that
his broken English and mispelled words were really charming.

--------------letter from Juris follows-----------------------------------
Riga, 7.11.93

Dear Amara,

thanks for your small, but nice letter I have received a month ago.  This time
I was going to Germany for EUROLAS meeting and had no time to write you some
words.  Of course we have sent already a short letter to your collegue who has
helped us with a scietifique journals.

I'm realy glad on your success with Masters degree and your happy possibility
to continue scientifique activities at Ames.  Here in Latvia we have a lot of
problems with space sciences.  And perhaps the most negative result of our 
independence is destruction of scientifique structures we have had before. Of 
course, our life now is much more better in nearly all aspects, but for us
the lost of the state support for science (specialy for astronomy and space 
science) is with exstreme importance.  And as result of this some degradation
of education system is going be started too. The best scientists are going 
abroad, some others to privat companies dealing mainly with... commerce.

The explanation to this is quite simple: the former times we were envolved 
in many russian military science projects having a strong financial coverige.
We have developed a lot of institutions and labs with quite reasonable 
scientifique level, because the military science in Russia was perhaps the
only corresponding to the world standarts. But now this potential is out of 
value for Latvia and we are loosing the possibility to use it in the favor
of the world community because of funds and ignorance from the governement.
Unfortunately with an independence we have got also a some kind of wild 
capitalism having no interest to science, education and culture too. The
highest values for our todays society are privat property, commerce and ...
nearly no more.  For the begining this is perhaps not so bad, but I hope
very much, that it will be changed for more in the future.  The life in the
time of the social changements is of course interesting, full of emotions
and events, but not favorable for science.  It is the same in all
east-european countries I know.  But we are still alive, we are trying
to work and in some respects we have even succeeded.

Thank you for invitation to contact with you if I'll be in San Francisko Bay
area.  I have no plans and opportunity to do it in nearest future, but who 
knows. The life is very unpredicatable for us this exiting and hopefull time.

We hope to see you in Riga too and, of course, speking little bit latvian....

Best regards, Your friend

Juris Zagars



