Date: Fri, 17 Dec 1993 03:50:39 -0100
From: carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Subject: Speed of evolution (was Human Language)

Simon! writes:
>Jeff Fabijanic writes
>>I've seen people make this distinction before, but I have to wonder, is it
>>reasonable to make the assumption that we *aren't* biologically disposed to
>>reading and writing?
>All this makes sense in the abstract, but when you look at the facts, you get 
>a different picture: by all accounts, written language extends back over no 
>more than the past 10,000 years, whereas the existence of homo-sapiens as a 
>species is likely many, many times that duration. 

In discussions of the genetic bases of human nature, a general assumption
seems to be that humans evolved in the hunter-gatherer niche.  This was the
basis of Tim May's recent essay on why men and women are different.  One
also finds this assumption in the literature on the topic, and in Simon!'s
post above.  However, I think that signficant evolution could have occured
in the few millenia since the invention of agriculture and the literate
societies that agriculture promotes. 

The amount of change of gene frequency in a population depends not only on
how long you wait but also on the selection pressure applied to the gene. 
If the species is in an unchanging niche, all the really influential genes
will have come to equilibrium in the population, and change will be slow. 
However, if the species is suddenly placed under a new selction pressure,
change can be swift.  You can double the frequency of a gene in only
seventy generations if the gene causes a mere 1% increase in the number of
surviving offspring.  In two hundred generations (about what it's been
since the invention of writing) the frequency of a gene can change
eightfold under that pressure.  If the pressure on the number of offspring
is larger, the doubling time is proportionally shorter.

I suggest that the invention of agriculture and everything that came with
it-- trade, government, writing, hard labor, villages, epidemics-- have
placed radically new selection pressures on the human species, and we have
evolved significantly in the last four thousand years.  (When I say "we
have evolved", I don't mean that we're a new species, just that certain
gene frequencies are quite different from what they were among our
neolithic ancestors, or from what they are among preagricultural people
today.)

What kind of people have more descendants in agricultural societies? 
Answer: men of the upper classes.  This seems to be a general fact about
most societies.   There's an article on the topic in last month's Brain &
Behavior journal, where I'm getting all my facts.  There have been at least
a dozen studies, examining societies as diverse as 18th-century England,
early 20th-century Sweden, and Stone Age New Guinea highlanders, and in all
cases upper-class males have more surviving offspring than lower-class
males, usually by a factor of two or more.  (It doesn't seem to be true for
us, late 20th-century industrial America, thanks to contraception and
monogamy, but that doesn't matter to the rest of my argument).

Because of this immense selection pressure, if there were a gene that
tended to promote its bearers into the upper classes, or keep them there if
they were already, that gene would spread through the population like
wildfire.  

So far I've been talking about gene frequencies in general, but now I'm
going to respond directly to Simon!'s post which argued against evolution
for literacy.

The nature of "the upper classes" varies radically from culture to culture,
but in some cultures, a man could move into the upper classes by the
ability to read and write, or by doing things for which reading and writing
were a prerequisite.  In such societies, genes that promoted dyslexia or
short attention span would be selected against.  

I can think of two cultures in which the upper class was open to those who
showed unusual talent in literacy.  There are lots of cultures where the
upper classes were expected to read and write, and the commoners weren't,
but in these two promotion was based largely on literary skills.  

The first example is China.  Confucius (500bc?) came up with the idea of
the civil service exam.  Since then, Chinese government has generally been
staffed by men who could compose essays and poetry on an assigned subject. 
And in China, joining the govenment was the way for ambitious commoners to
get ahead.  (I know, this is a large generalization.  There were lots of
times when it wasn't true.  But I would venture to say it was true for at
least a thousand out of the last two thousand years.)  

The second example is Ashkenazic (Eastern and Central European) Jewry. 
>From at least the twelfth century to the present day, this culture has held
men of learning in the highest regard.  Mastery of immense texts is a
prerequisite for becoming a rabbi.  There are many tales of boys of the
lowest classes, who by demonstrating extraordinary logical and literary
powers, rose to positions of importance.  In a culture without a military,
hereditary nobility, or a government hierarchy, this was an important way
to get ahead in the world. 

I think it would be interesting to measure the frequency of dyslexia among
people descended from these two cultures, and see if it was different from
that among people descended from areas that didn't have writing until
recently, like Hawaii.  You'd have to standardize the culture these various
people were brought up in, but America is pretty good at standardizing
culture.


--carlf

