From: jbaker@halcyon.com (James Baker)
Subject: Vision Improvement; Biofeedback

    I have read several comments on the list regarding myopia, vision
improvement, eye exercises, genetics-and-eyeball-elongation etc. I did some
research in this area in 1988 after I had the idea for a simple biofeedback
training gadget for eye exercises. I discovered that I wasn't the first to
think of this and that a fellow in New York named Joe Tractman <sp?> sells
a machine under the trade name Accomotrac <sp?> that does essentially the
same thing. Tractman has lots of patents on his machine. They make
interesting reading. They also helped convince me not to pursue my plans
further. 

   My guess was that eye exercises are harder than necessary and less
successful than they could be because there is no way to determine if all
that muscular activity is going to the muscles used for focus. Imagine
trying to strengthen your index fingers by doing pushups. It would probably
work somewhat but there is a better way. 

    I'm making the assumption (in engineering terms), that mis-focus is
caused by an habitual offset in the focus feedback loop, not by the eye
geometry. If this is in fact true, matters less than the whether the
approach can be made to work. We could learn to tense or relax the muscles
used for focus by looking into the machine and listening to a tone that
corresponds to eyeball focal length. (Relaxing the  muscles focuses the
eye's lens farther; tensing the muscles focuses nearer.) The user would
learn to make the tone go lower or higher, (lower for nearsighted people,
higher for farsighted people). 

   I heard that Accomotrac machines are popular with the Air Force. There's
this problem that occurs when pilots spend too many hours in simulators
watching a horizon projected on a screen 30 feet away. They forget how to
spot things on the real horizon. 

   Accomotrac machines cost $20K or more. I spent a few minutes (at $75/hr)
and learned nothing (except that I couldn't afford it). 


