Subject: death, shmeath, just so it's not permanent

I'm not sure whether my point wasn't clear, or whether no one wanted
to reply to it, but on the chance that it's the former, I'm going
to repeat myself more clearly.

I think that adolescents in a post-Singularity culture might
rebel by risking *non-reversable* death. In fact, you could
have an interesting sf "Groundhog Day" story based on the
idea. Teen kills hirself w/o current backup--the parent recreates
the teen using a surreptitiously kept backup, and the same
thing happens. The parent keeps using earlier and earlier
backups until sie realizes that sie's the problem and needs
to change sie's behavior. (Though with a technology like that,
sie might edit the child....maybe sie has a religious/philosophical
objection to editing people. I can easily see editing people
being more stigmatized than recreating them.)

A vaguely related question: If the only way that you could get
the learning speed of a three-year old was by spending a while
as a three-year old (say, in order to become a "native speaker"
of more languages), would you do it?

Nancy Lebovitz

